[?25l> Now I understand the program. It takes two command-line arguments, creates a struct, uses container_of macro to find the parent struct from member pointers, then prints the sum of a + b. The container_of logic is a no-op for member a (offset 0) and subtracts sizeof(int) for member b.

In practice, find_container_of_a(&t.a) returns &t and find_container_of_b(&t.b) also returns &t, so the output is just a + b. The C code also has no bounds checking on argv — if fewer than 2 args are given, it's UB (accessing argv out of bounds). atoi returns 0 for non-numeric strings.
